WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:02.020
It’s the year 478 BCE.

00:00:02.020 --> 00:00:08.550
And an interesting embassy of various diplomats
presents itself to the Athenian Assembly.

00:00:08.550 --> 00:00:12.499
This video is sponsored by the Great Courses
Plus but more on that later.

00:00:12.499 --> 00:00:16.699
This embassy that presented itself before
the Athenian Assembly, well actually they

00:00:16.699 --> 00:00:20.939
would first come before the Prytaneis who
where part of the Boule who would decide whether

00:00:20.939 --> 00:00:24.640
to present them before the Assembly but the
Athenian government is all but not simple

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:29.669
so for simplicity sake let's just say the
embassy presented itself before the Assembly.

00:00:29.669 --> 00:00:34.739
This embassy was a coalition of various Greek
Ionian city states, but also most likely other

00:00:34.739 --> 00:00:36.910
Greek city states were part of it as well.

00:00:36.910 --> 00:00:41.579
And they all came to Athens, to present before
the Athenians, their plea for Athens to take

00:00:41.579 --> 00:00:44.870
the leadership of the Hellenic Alliance away
from the Spartans.

00:00:44.870 --> 00:00:50.090
Why is there a Hellenic Alliance, why are
the Spartans leading it, why are the Aionians

00:00:50.090 --> 00:00:54.379
asking Athens to help, and how is this all
even related to the creation of the Athenian

00:00:54.379 --> 00:00:55.379
Empire?

00:00:55.379 --> 00:00:56.809
Well let's get into it.

00:00:56.809 --> 00:00:57.809
…

00:00:57.809 --> 00:01:01.329
Before 478 the Hellenic world was embroiled
in a conflict the scale of which they have

00:01:01.329 --> 00:01:02.469
never seen before.

00:01:02.469 --> 00:01:07.640
The Persian Empire ruling over most of the
eastern known world had set it sights on Greece,

00:01:07.640 --> 00:01:12.480
and so the conflict known as the Greco-Persian
Wars started, I do not intend to cover this

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:16.850
entire topic as that would require an entire
video series of its own but it is important

00:01:16.850 --> 00:01:20.860
to mention certain events from it that lead
to the arrival of the aforementioned diplomatic

00:01:20.860 --> 00:01:23.670
embassy to Athens in 478.

00:01:23.670 --> 00:01:25.590
So the simplified version goes like this.

00:01:25.590 --> 00:01:31.270
The Greco Persian Wars started with a rebellion
of Ionian Greeks against the Persian rule

00:01:31.270 --> 00:01:32.270
in 499.

00:01:32.270 --> 00:01:35.729
They were supported in this rebellion by Athenians
and Eretrians.

00:01:35.729 --> 00:01:41.009
The revolt was subdued by the Persians in
493 but the Persian king Darius vowed to make

00:01:41.009 --> 00:01:44.689
Athens and Eretria pay for their support of
the revolt.

00:01:44.689 --> 00:01:49.750
And so with this in mind he invaded Greece
in 490 where he was promptly defeated at Marathon

00:01:49.750 --> 00:01:54.310
by mainly Athenian but also some Eretrain
forces led by Callimachus.

00:01:54.310 --> 00:01:59.680
During the battle Callimachus was supported
by 10 strategoi, basically Athenian Generals.

00:01:59.680 --> 00:02:05.869
The most important of which were Aristides,
Themistocles, Xanthipus and, of course Miltiades.

00:02:05.869 --> 00:02:10.780
Militiades was the one to pressure the Athenians
to face the Persians in open battle and he

00:02:10.780 --> 00:02:14.880
was the one who devised the battle tactic
that defeated the Persians even though they

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:16.790
outnumbered the Athenians.

00:02:16.790 --> 00:02:21.500
After the battle the story goes that the remaining
Persians wanted to use their fleet to attack

00:02:21.500 --> 00:02:24.700
Athens since the Athenian army was in Marathon.

00:02:24.700 --> 00:02:29.850
But the Athenians marched as fast as they
could in full armour, 40 km from Marathon

00:02:29.850 --> 00:02:34.861
to Athens, just in time to see the Persian
fleet arriving, who upon seeing the Athenian

00:02:34.861 --> 00:02:37.530
army in the city, decided to retreat.

00:02:37.530 --> 00:02:42.480
This story was later romanticized, and confused
with a different story, resulting in the story

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:43.520
we know today.

00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:48.290
Which is that after the Athenian victory at
Marathon a herald called Philippides was dispatched

00:02:48.290 --> 00:02:50.510
to announce the news in Athens.

00:02:50.510 --> 00:02:56.770
He ran the full 40 km in armour exclaimed
before the Assembly “We've won!” and promptly

00:02:56.770 --> 00:02:58.390
died of exhaustion.

00:02:58.390 --> 00:03:04.270
This story is why today’s marathon is called
a Marathon and why it is 40 km long, well

00:03:04.270 --> 00:03:06.410
not exactly, but I digress.

00:03:06.410 --> 00:03:10.930
After the Persian defeat at Marathon a year
later Miltiades led the Athenian troops on

00:03:10.930 --> 00:03:15.950
the offensive but this proved to be not so
successful and he even managed to get grievously

00:03:15.950 --> 00:03:17.870
injured during this campaign.

00:03:17.870 --> 00:03:23.720
His rivals in Athens used this failed campaign,
and other arguments, to get Miltiades imprisoned.

00:03:23.720 --> 00:03:29.040
This man who helped Athens defeat the Persians
at Marathon died due to his injuries while

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:31.020
being imprisoned by said Athenians.

00:03:31.020 --> 00:03:36.640
He was also almost ostracized if he had lived
a couple of years longer to see this new law

00:03:36.640 --> 00:03:37.720
come into practice.

00:03:37.720 --> 00:03:39.030
What is Ostracism?

00:03:39.030 --> 00:03:43.060
Well for those who don’t know ostracism
was an Athenian way of getting rid of people

00:03:43.060 --> 00:03:45.620
who might be aspiring to overthrow the democracy.

00:03:45.620 --> 00:03:50.900
If more than 6000 votes were casted, who's
ever name was mentioned the most in those

00:03:50.900 --> 00:03:55.080
6000+ votes was exiled from Athens for a period
of 10 years.

00:03:55.080 --> 00:03:58.940
The problem with this was that even though
the reason why the ostracism was introduced

00:03:58.940 --> 00:04:03.940
was quite altruistic, it just ended up being
yet another way of getting rid of your political

00:04:03.940 --> 00:04:09.910
rivals and almost anyone who became popular
in Athenian politics got ostracized eventually.

00:04:09.910 --> 00:04:15.480
Even the person who introduced ostracism and
reformed Athenian democracy to be more “democractic”,

00:04:15.480 --> 00:04:21.150
Cleisthenes, is said to have been ostracized
himself, maybe the sources are unclear, but

00:04:21.150 --> 00:04:23.780
it is known that his son was later ostracised.

00:04:23.780 --> 00:04:28.590
So a little foreshadowing there of how it
ends for most of the people in these videos.

00:04:28.590 --> 00:04:33.430
Going back to the Greco-Persian Wars, Darius
was preparing another invasion of Greece but

00:04:33.430 --> 00:04:37.220
died in 486 and was succeeded by his son Xerxes.

00:04:37.220 --> 00:04:42.320
Xerxes had to deal with rebellions and consolidate
his power within Persia and due to this he

00:04:42.320 --> 00:04:46.290
put his father’s planned Greek invasion
on hold until 480.

00:04:46.290 --> 00:04:50.590
During this 10 year interbellum in the war,
two prominent politicians came to ascendents

00:04:50.590 --> 00:04:51.590
in Athens.

00:04:51.590 --> 00:04:55.900
The veterans of the Battle at Marathon Themistocles
and Aristides.

00:04:55.900 --> 00:05:00.250
Themistocles was popular with the lower classes
mainly because he came from them, he was known

00:05:00.250 --> 00:05:05.760
as a cunning man who always had a plan and
to whom sometimes the ends did justify the

00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:06.760
means.

00:05:06.760 --> 00:05:10.690
Aristides on the other hand positioned himself
against Themistocles, he was the champion

00:05:10.690 --> 00:05:16.760
of the upper classes, called “The just Aristides”
by his supporters, he was incorruptible, conservative

00:05:16.760 --> 00:05:21.280
and as Plutarch puts it, he was in politics
“to secure the best results consistent with

00:05:21.280 --> 00:05:24.460
safety and righteousness” not to be a populist.

00:05:24.460 --> 00:05:29.310
Nonetheless the main issue these two disagreed
on was whether Athens should focus more on

00:05:29.310 --> 00:05:34.900
its navy or its military, with Themistocles
supporting the former and Aristides the latter.

00:05:34.900 --> 00:05:40.730
This heated debate culminated in 483 when
a very rich seam of silver was found in the

00:05:40.730 --> 00:05:43.150
Athenian controlled mines Laurion.

00:05:43.150 --> 00:05:47.420
Themistocles wanted to use the new found money
to build a great new navy for Athens, while

00:05:47.420 --> 00:05:51.520
Aristidis fearing this would mean Athen’s
abandonment of its military in favor of the

00:05:51.520 --> 00:05:55.450
navy proposed dividing up the money amongst
the Athenian citizens.

00:05:55.450 --> 00:05:59.580
At the end it looked like a compromise was
going to be reached between the two sides

00:05:59.580 --> 00:06:02.330
but an ostracism was held in this time.

00:06:02.330 --> 00:06:07.510
During the ostracism an illiterate man came
up to Aristides not recognizing him.

00:06:07.510 --> 00:06:13.060
He asked Aristides if he could please write
the name Aristides on his pottery shard (which

00:06:13.060 --> 00:06:18.480
was the ballot), confused Aristides asked
the man “has Aristides wronged you at any

00:06:18.480 --> 00:06:24.030
point” to which the man replied “No, and
I do not even know him, but it irritates me

00:06:24.030 --> 00:06:26.780
to hear him everywhere called 'the Just”.

00:06:26.780 --> 00:06:31.530
Aristides than true to his nickname wrote
his own name on the pottery shard.

00:06:31.530 --> 00:06:37.050
Unsurprisingly Aristides was ostracized, meaning
Themistoclese won and Athens promptly started

00:06:37.050 --> 00:06:39.800
to build one of the largest fleets in the
Aegean.

00:06:39.800 --> 00:06:45.550
In 481, Greeks learned of the planned Persian
invasion and so called for a Greek Council.

00:06:45.550 --> 00:06:51.040
There were around 700 Greek city states at
the time but only 70 showed up to the council.

00:06:51.040 --> 00:06:55.810
However even with that it has to be stated
how big of a deal this is, even 10% of the

00:06:55.810 --> 00:06:59.870
Greek city states showing up to a council
when some of them are technically in an active

00:06:59.870 --> 00:07:01.820
war with each other is impressive.

00:07:01.820 --> 00:07:07.190
The council decided a Hellenic Alliance with
the power to levy troops and send ambassadors

00:07:07.190 --> 00:07:09.690
would be formed to fight the Persians.

00:07:09.690 --> 00:07:14.690
This Hellenic Alliance promptly made the Spartans
the main leaders of the alliance and of the

00:07:14.690 --> 00:07:19.030
joined military, because I mean it's the war
obsessed Spartans who else should lead the

00:07:19.030 --> 00:07:20.240
allies to war.

00:07:20.240 --> 00:07:24.240
The Athenians, on the other hand with their
fancy new big navy, and due to some clever

00:07:24.240 --> 00:07:29.150
diplomatic maneuvering by Themistocles, managed
to be appointed as the leaders of the Allied

00:07:29.150 --> 00:07:30.150
navy.

00:07:30.150 --> 00:07:32.810
And so with Athenian Themistocles leading
the Navy and

00:07:32.810 --> 00:07:38.690
Spartan Leonidas the military
what follows next are the stories of legends.

00:07:38.690 --> 00:07:43.669
Persians win at Thermopylae killing Leonidas
and march to Attica to burn down Athens along

00:07:43.669 --> 00:07:46.760
with a bunch of other cities and temples along
the way.

00:07:46.760 --> 00:07:51.260
Most Athenians however retreated with the
Hellenic Alliance to the Isthmus of Corinth

00:07:51.260 --> 00:07:52.980
so they were safe for now.

00:07:52.980 --> 00:07:57.639
Also Athens during this time passed a law
of recalling exiles, meaning Aristides could

00:07:57.639 --> 00:08:03.000
return and so he did, promptly serving under
Themistocles in the Allied Navy.

00:08:03.000 --> 00:08:07.400
Also since Leonidas was dead he was promptly
succeeded in the leadership of the allies

00:08:07.400 --> 00:08:09.080
by another spartan Pausanias.

00:08:09.080 --> 00:08:14.960
The Persian navy was then defeated thanks
to Themistocles cunning at the Battle of Salamis.

00:08:14.960 --> 00:08:18.840
After that the allies went on the offencive
defeating the invading forces at the Battle

00:08:18.840 --> 00:08:22.350
of Plataea and the remaining Persian Navy
at the Battle of Mycale.

00:08:22.350 --> 00:08:27.810
During the Battle of Platea Aristidis showed
his confidence, orator skills, and honor,

00:08:27.810 --> 00:08:31.100
even helping to put down a conspiracy within
the army.

00:08:31.100 --> 00:08:36.260
These actions gave the allies great admiration
for Aristides but more on that later.

00:08:36.260 --> 00:08:40.050
With the Persian threat no longer imminent
Athenians returned to their burned downed

00:08:40.050 --> 00:08:41.360
and destroyed city.

00:08:41.360 --> 00:08:46.010
Athenians started to rebuild their city along
with the destroyed walls, naturally the Spartans

00:08:46.010 --> 00:08:49.931
objected to the Athenians rebuilding their
walls because well they didn’t want their

00:08:49.931 --> 00:08:52.310
main rivals to have walls.

00:08:52.310 --> 00:08:56.990
Themistocles however, ever the trickster,
devised a plan of how to trick the Spartans.

00:08:56.990 --> 00:09:01.380
He told the Athenians to focus on only building
the walls as quick as they can.

00:09:01.380 --> 00:09:06.190
He then headed an embassy to Sparta along
with a man named Kimon where he delayed for

00:09:06.190 --> 00:09:07.870
as long as he could.

00:09:07.870 --> 00:09:12.140
Spartans however didn’t believe his words
and so Themistocles suggested the Spartans

00:09:12.140 --> 00:09:17.210
send envoys of their own, to see for themselves
if Athenians are building the walls or not.

00:09:17.210 --> 00:09:22.270
He then himself send a herald to Athens, telling
them to keep hostage the Spartan envoys that

00:09:22.270 --> 00:09:23.270
will arrive.

00:09:23.270 --> 00:09:28.250
The Athenians did so and when Aristides arrived
to Sparta to enform Themistocles that the

00:09:28.250 --> 00:09:33.810
walls have been finished, enraged Spartans
wanted to imprison the entire Athenian embassy

00:09:33.810 --> 00:09:39.740
to which Themistocles nicely pointed out they
had the Spartan envoys in custody in Athens.

00:09:39.740 --> 00:09:45.240
So the Spartans reluctantly released the Athenian
Embassy and the Athenians released the Spartan

00:09:45.240 --> 00:09:46.240
embassy.

00:09:46.240 --> 00:09:50.710
Before leaving, Themistocles informed the
Spartans for future reference that Athenians

00:09:50.710 --> 00:09:55.130
weren’t just any Greek city the Spartans
could boss around and in fact it was a city

00:09:55.130 --> 00:09:59.980
of distinguished people which could make their
own decisions without Spartan or any other

00:09:59.980 --> 00:10:04.390
city’s consent.

00:10:04.390 --> 00:10:09.380
The new Athenian Walls on top of encompassing
a much larger area than the actual city itself

00:10:09.380 --> 00:10:13.050
where also build around the new Athenian port
Piraeus.

00:10:13.050 --> 00:10:16.670
Themistocles couldn’t stress enough how
valuable having access to the sea was for

00:10:16.670 --> 00:10:20.520
the Athenians now that they were the dominant
naval power in the Greek world.

00:10:20.520 --> 00:10:26.070
He even suggested that if worse comes to worse,
the Athenians abandoned their city in favor

00:10:26.070 --> 00:10:27.730
of defending Piraeus.

00:10:27.730 --> 00:10:31.510
Whether that will ever happen, I guess we’ll
just have to see.

00:10:31.510 --> 00:10:35.791
Going back to the Greco Persian Wars the Hellenic
Alliance continued being on the offensive

00:10:35.791 --> 00:10:39.950
taking over Gallipoli, raiding Cyprus and
capturing Byzantium.

00:10:39.950 --> 00:10:44.500
All while most of the Ionian Greeks, remember
them, rebelled from the Persians and joined

00:10:44.500 --> 00:10:45.990
the Alliance as well.

00:10:45.990 --> 00:10:50.500
However, during this entire time, the Spartans
where the ones in charge of the alliances

00:10:50.500 --> 00:10:51.500
actions.

00:10:51.500 --> 00:10:55.050
After all as mentioned before you’re going
to war with the largest Empire in the known

00:10:55.050 --> 00:11:00.470
world, so you probably want to make the war
obsessed Spartans the leaders of the Alliance.

00:11:00.470 --> 00:11:05.020
But now that the imminent threat of Persian
invasion of Greece has been largely stifled,

00:11:05.020 --> 00:11:08.839
the problems of Spartan hegemony are starting
to come to light.

00:11:08.839 --> 00:11:14.120
The Spartan leader of the alliance Pausanias
was said to be dictatorial, violent, corrupt

00:11:14.120 --> 00:11:16.110
and even often irrational.

00:11:16.110 --> 00:11:21.300
Now these statements may have been just Athenian
propaganda at play but considering that Spartans

00:11:21.300 --> 00:11:25.930
were known for carrying sticks and beating
with them anyone who disagreed with their

00:11:25.930 --> 00:11:30.990
opinion, it would be easy to assume that Pausanias
wasn’t really liked by the allied troops.

00:11:30.990 --> 00:11:36.300
Which brings us back to 478 and the allied
embassy coming to Athens, pleading for them

00:11:36.300 --> 00:11:39.220
to take over the leadership of the Alliance.

00:11:39.220 --> 00:11:43.370
Athenians agreed to take the leadership away
from the Spartans under certain conditions.

00:11:43.370 --> 00:11:47.649
The exact details of this and how the new
alliance was runned is a matter of debate

00:11:47.649 --> 00:11:52.600
because no sources talk about it in detail,
but to present a coherent story I am going

00:11:52.600 --> 00:11:53.930
to go with N.G.L.

00:11:53.930 --> 00:11:55.459
Hammonds representation.

00:11:55.459 --> 00:12:00.390
Each ally will swear an oath to the gods to
have an offencive and a defensive alliance

00:12:00.390 --> 00:12:04.490
with Athens. (also sometimes to the alliance
itself as a whole, depends)

00:12:04.490 --> 00:12:08.089
They will also be required to participate
in the alliance’s council on the island

00:12:08.089 --> 00:12:09.089
of Delos.

00:12:09.089 --> 00:12:14.270
Each ally has a seat and one vote on the council,
however the Athenian vote is equivalent to

00:12:14.270 --> 00:12:16.580
the whole alliances council vote.

00:12:16.580 --> 00:12:21.020
Plus Athens will hold a permanent Hegemony
over the leadership of the alliance’s navy

00:12:21.020 --> 00:12:22.020
and army.

00:12:22.020 --> 00:12:26.100
On top of that any booty gotten from raiding
and pillaging the Persian territory will be

00:12:26.100 --> 00:12:30.170
divided amongst the allies and of course 1/10
of it will go to the gods.

00:12:30.170 --> 00:12:35.410
Lastly each alli has to either support the
alliance in military/naval aid or financial

00:12:35.410 --> 00:12:38.460
payments which would be held at a treasury
in Delos.

00:12:38.460 --> 00:12:39.910
Why Delos you ask?

00:12:39.910 --> 00:12:44.120
Well partly because it’s conveniently placed
in the middle of the Aegean, partly because

00:12:44.120 --> 00:12:48.490
it’s an island, and so easily defendable
by the Athenian Navy, and partly because it

00:12:48.490 --> 00:12:51.800
is a very religiously important site in the
Hellenic world.

00:12:51.800 --> 00:12:57.209
It is the island where the twin gods of Apollo
and Artemis were born and came down from the

00:12:57.209 --> 00:12:58.220
mountain Cynthus.

00:12:58.220 --> 00:13:02.960
Having the allies meet at such a religious
important place would make them think twice

00:13:02.960 --> 00:13:07.100
before breaking the oath they swore to Athens
before the gods.

00:13:07.100 --> 00:13:12.240
Most allies mainly the islanders and the Ionians
agreed to these terms while others like Sparta

00:13:12.240 --> 00:13:13.720
just left the alliance.

00:13:13.720 --> 00:13:18.390
Why Sparta gave up the hegemony of the Alliance
is uncertain but it is mostly agreed that

00:13:18.390 --> 00:13:22.779
they felt like the Persians have been soundly
defeated and there was no longer a reason

00:13:22.779 --> 00:13:24.980
to continue on with the Alliance.

00:13:24.980 --> 00:13:28.750
This combined with the Athenian restructuring
of the alliance meant that not everybody was

00:13:28.750 --> 00:13:30.910
happy being part of the alliance anymore.

00:13:30.910 --> 00:13:34.180
Why did other allies agree to the terms set
by Athens?

00:13:34.180 --> 00:13:38.900
Well for the Aioninas and some islanders this
was a perfect deal as they were in a constant

00:13:38.900 --> 00:13:43.399
threat from Persia and so being part of such
an alliance with one of the most powerful

00:13:43.399 --> 00:13:45.740
cities in the Greek world meant protection.

00:13:45.740 --> 00:13:50.520
Other cities even at this early point may
have been strongarmed by Athens in to joining

00:13:50.520 --> 00:13:53.660
the new alliance but we have no way of knowing
that for sure.

00:13:53.660 --> 00:13:56.160
Lastly there’s an economic aspect to it.

00:13:56.160 --> 00:14:01.470
Being part of the alliance means protection,
stability and more streamlined access to markets,

00:14:01.470 --> 00:14:05.399
something which the trade oriented Thassos
would enjoy.

00:14:05.399 --> 00:14:11.230
So in 478 the Athenians became the leaders
of a newly created alliance which henceforth

00:14:11.230 --> 00:14:16.830
we shall call the Delian league, a political
entity which gradually over the course of

00:14:16.830 --> 00:14:19.890
the 5th century became the Athenian Empire.

00:14:19.890 --> 00:14:24.651
So now that Athens is leading this new Delian
League what are they going to do about the

00:14:24.651 --> 00:14:29.790
Persian threat, also what is Themistocles
and Aristides role in this new league, plus

00:14:29.790 --> 00:14:34.050
who was that new guy named Kimon who helped
Themistocles trick the Spartans?

00:14:34.050 --> 00:14:38.670
All very good questions that are going to
be answered in the next video of my Athenian

00:14:38.670 --> 00:14:40.420
Empire Series.

00:14:40.420 --> 00:14:44.410
However if you can’t wait for that video
because let's be honest ‘I’ don’t even

00:14:44.410 --> 00:14:48.540
know when the next video in this series is
going to come out, you can always go to the

00:14:48.540 --> 00:14:52.140
Great Courses Plus and go on a deep dive of
Athenian history.

00:14:52.140 --> 00:14:56.360
